  about: [
    `<em>Hamlet</em> is the longest of Shakespeare's plays, the most-quoted, and the strangest. A young man comes home from university to find his father dead and his uncle on the throne, married to his mother. Then his father's ghost arrives with the news that the uncle murdered him and demands revenge. The rest of the play is what Hamlet does with that information — which is, mostly, talk. He talks to himself. He talks to the audience. He talks his way around the simple act of killing the man, four acts long, until the killing finally happens in five minutes at the end and takes most of the cast with it.`,
    `What makes the play strange is that the famous indecision is also the play's argument. Hamlet does not just delay; he is conscious of delaying, sees himself delaying, has an inner life big enough to hold the watching of his own paralysis. Shakespeare invents — or refines past anything earlier — a kind of literary character who can think about themselves. The play has been read for four centuries since as the first portrait of the modern self.`,
  ],
  chaptersSubtitle: 'All 20 scenes, by act and beat — from the ghost on the wall to the duel that kills everyone.',
  chaptersLead: `<p>Hamlet is structured as five acts of unequal length. Act I is the haunting and the charge. Act II is what Hamlet does with it (and what others do with him). Act III is the explosion: the play-within-a-play, the prayer scene, the killing of Polonius. Act IV is the long aftermath — Hamlet sent away, Ophelia broken, Laertes returned. Act V is the graveyard and the duel. Twenty scenes total.</p>`,
  themesByline: 'Five threads through the play',
  themesLead: `Hamlet is a revenge tragedy that wants to be more than a revenge tragedy. The plays it grew out of would have ended in the killing in Act II. Shakespeare keeps writing for three more acts, and these are what he is writing about.`,

